        <text x="420" y="211" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#94a3b8" text-anchor="middle">Standard Winter Time (Apr - Oct)</text>

        <rect x="590" y="195" width="160" height="24" fill="#f59e0b" fill-opacity="0.4" stroke="#f59e0b" rx="4" />
        <text x="670" y="211" font-family="sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#fbbf24" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">DST Active (Oct - Dec)</text>
      </svg>
      <p class="infographic-caption">Figure 1: Seasonal inversion of Daylight Saving Time active periods between the Northern and Southern Hemispheres.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 3 -->
  <section id="regional-rules" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Regional Transition Rules">
    <h2>3. Regional Transition Rules &amp; Mathematical Algorithms</h2>
    <p>
      Rather than specifying hardcoded static calendar dates, timekeeping regulations rely on positional calendar rules:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>United States &amp; Canada Rule</strong>: Established by the Energy Policy Act of 2005. DST starts on the <em>second Sunday in March</em> and ends on the <em>first Sunday in November</em>. For details on North American timezone structures, explore our <a href="/usa-time-zones/">USA time zones guide</a> and <a href="/canada-time-zones/">Canada time zones guide</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>European Union &amp; UK Rule</strong>: Codified under EU Directive 2000/84/EC. Summer Time starts on the <em>last Sunday in March</em> and ends on the <em>last Sunday in October</em>. Learn more in our <a href="/bst-to-est/">BST to EST converter</a> and <a href="/cet-to-est/">CET to EST guide</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Australia Southern Rule</strong>: Follows state legislation. DST starts on the <em>first Sunday in October</em> and ends on the <em>first Sunday in April</em>. Review full breakdown in our <a href="/australia-time-zones/">Australia time zones guide</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 4 -->
  <section id="southern-hemisphere" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Southern Hemisphere DST Cycle">
    <h2>4. Southern Hemisphere DST Cycle (Reversed Seasons)</h2>
    <p>
      In the Southern Hemisphere, summer spans December, January, and February. Consequently, regions observing DST set their clocks forward in September or October and return to standard time in April.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, Sydney, Australia operates on Australian Eastern Standard Time (<a href="/aest-to-est/">AEST, UTC+10</a>) during winter. In October, Sydney advances to Australian Eastern Daylight Time (AEDT, UTC+11). When converting between Sydney and New York, the total time difference shifts from 14 hours in June to 16 hours in December, or 15 hours during transition gap weeks.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 5 -->
  <section id="three-week-gap" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="The Three-Week Gap">
    <h2>5. The "Three-Week Gap" Between US and EU Clock Shifts</h2>
    <p>
      Because the United States moves to DST on the second Sunday of March while Europe shifts on the last Sunday of March, a temporary 2-to-3 week gap occurs every spring. A matching 1-week gap occurs in autumn between late October and early November.
    </p>
    <p>
      During these transition gaps:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>The standard 5-hour time difference between London (<a href="/what-is-gmt/">GMT</a>) and New York (<a href="/what-is-est/">EST</a>) narrows to <strong>4 hours</strong> for 3 weeks in March. Check real-time values with our <a href="/gmt-to-est/">GMT to EST converter</a> and <a href="/gmt-vs-est/">GMT vs EST comparison</a>.</li>
      <li>Financial trading floor overlaps shift by 60 minutes, altering cross-market arbitrage windows between Wall Street and the London Stock Exchange.</li>
      <li>International flight departure schedules require careful double-checking against local civil clock arrival times.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 6 -->
  <section id="clock-change-mechanics" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="How Clocks Transition">
    <h2>6. How Clocks Transition: 2:00 AM Local vs. 01:00 UTC</h2>
    <p>
      The specific mechanism used to adjust clocks varies by region:
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>North America (Local Time Shift)</strong>: Clocks change sequentially across each time zone as local time hits 2:00 AM. Eastern Time changes first at 2:00 AM EST (shifting to 3:00 AM EDT), followed 1 hour later by Central Time (<a href="/gmt-to-cst/">CST to CDT</a>), Mountain Time, and Pacific Time (<a href="/gmt-to-pst/">PST to PDT</a>).
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Europe (Synchronized UTC Shift)</strong>: All European Union member states adjust simultaneously at <strong>01:00 UTC</strong> regardless of local timezone offset. Thus, London shifts at 1:00 AM local, Paris/Berlin at 2:00 AM local, and Athens/Helsinki at 3:00 AM local.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 7 -->
  <section id="multi-year-forecast" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Future Transition Rule Engine">
    <h2>7. Future Transition Rule Engine (2026–2030)</h2>
    <p>
      For forward-looking event planning and scheduling, review the predicted start and end dates for North America and Europe through 2030 in our dedicated <a href="/dst-calendar/">DST calendar guide</a>.
    </p>

    <div class="table-wrap">
